    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. It was not a bucket policy issue; it was a misunderstanding of the permissions model. The policy \"Version\", \"Statement\", and \"Effect\" parts indicated it was intended for a S3 bucket policy, not a Lambda function's execution role.\n\nA separate IAM policy attached to the Lambda function's execution role does not apply this S3 bucket policy directly. Instead, the Lambda function must have permissions to perform \"s3:PutObject\". This means that the Lambda function's execution role should have an \"Action\" statement with \"s3:PutObject\".\n\nEnsure that the Lambda execution role policy has the necessary permission statements like so:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\"],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis policy should be attached directly to the Lambda function's execution role, granting it permission to write log data to the specified S3 bucket. If there are other dependencies, such as Lambda invoking other AWS services, you'll need to add those to this policy",
      "source": "target",
