      "content": "It's common to get frustrated when permissions don't work as expected. You've covered most of the usual suspects, but let's consider a few more possibilities:\n\n1. **Lambda Service Control Policies (SCPs):** If you're operating under AWS Organizations, SCPs can enforce multi-account permissions. Check no SCP is placed on your account or organizational unit that might restrict Lambda function interactions with S3.\n\n2. **Prefix filter:** Sometimes Lambda logs written by built-in CloudWatch logging have default prefixes to prevent overwriting logs in S3. Ensure not to write logs under a prefix if CloudWatch logs are initially on CloudWatch before they're exported to S3.\n\n3. **Logical ID and Resource Specificity:** Confirm that the logical ID in your bucket policy, if you purposefully switched out \"my-app-logs-bucket\" to SCMP or a different mid-name for security purposes, matches the actual bucket name entirely.\n\n4. **Secrets Manager SSM Parameter Store or KMS keys:** Check for any Secrets Manager or KMS key permissions tied to the resource; however, these usually don't cause \"Access Denied\" errors unless you've somehow incorporated them into the Lambda function's execution policy, which is rare.\n\n5. **Role Credentials:** After deployment, check",
